Video stream: bound the A/V sync delay request before converting it to frames

A 1001<->1005 VP8 call stopped assembling pictures about 25 seconds in and never
recovered, while RTP kept arriving at ~160 pkt/s. The jitter buffer filled to its 590-frame
capacity with decodable pictures and handed out none of them. Measured on an SM-M146B,
2026-09-25.

The gate is in get_frame()'s scan loop:

    if (++frm_cnt == stream->dec_delay_cnt + stream->dec_add_delay_cnt)

frm_cnt counts DISTINCT frame timestamps walked in the jitter buffer, and the sum is the
minimum-delay target. At the stall the target read 1,616,896 -- more distinct timestamps
than the buffer can ever hold -- so the equality was unreachable and got_frame stayed
false for the rest of the call.

dec_delay_cnt was 5 throughout; it is clamped to jb max_count * 4/5 at both of its write
sites. dec_add_delay_cnt was 1,616,891, set once, by the av-sync adjustment. Its inputs,
captured at the transition:

    req=-59512786ms  fps=45/1  cur=111ms  target=-59512675ms  target_cnt=1616896

which reproduces exactly:

    target_delay_ms * dec_max_fps.num = -59,512,675 * 45 = -2,678,070,375   (< INT_MIN)
    wraps to                                              +1,616,896,921
    / denum / 1000                                      =  1,616,896

Two defects meet here. The arithmetic is done in signed 32-bit, and dec_max_fps.num is the
RTP clock rate (90000) whenever the learned frame rate was not an exact divisor of it, so
`ms * num` reaches 2^31 at about 23.9 seconds of delay. And the guard meant to catch this,

    if (target_delay_ms > 5000) { ... revert ... }

is one-sided and runs AFTER the conversion, so a large negative request never reaches it
and the overflow has already happened by the time it is tested.

This patch bounds the request before any conversion, in both directions, against the same
5000 ms the one-sided check already intended (now named
PJMEDIA_VID_STREAM_MAX_DELAY_ADJ_MSEC); does the ms-to-frames arithmetic in 64-bit; and
clamps the result to jb max_count * 4/5 before narrowing -- the bound dec_delay_cnt already
respects and dec_add_delay_cnt never did. Requests inside the policy are applied exactly as
before. The original upper-bound check is kept, now reachable only by an admitted request.

The `==` is deliberately left alone. frm_cnt is a local starting at zero, incremented by
exactly one per new timestamp and tested immediately with a break, so it cannot overshoot
within a scan; the defect was never the comparison but a target the buffer could not reach.

Where the absurd request comes from is NOT fixed here, and is instrumented instead. The
raw chain, captured at the event:

    raw[Audio] clk=16000 ref_ts=2177760970 pts=709120
               diff=2117915446 (0x7e3cd336)
               ntp_add=568523598516453 -> last_ntp=+132367s (36.77 h)
    lag[Audio] max_ntp=... (owner=Audio) ms_diff=132367664 (36.77 h)

The audio RTCP-SR references carry two independent RTP timelines under one SSRC
(0xbb2206e3): a main series near 2.177e9 advancing at 16000/s, and a sporadic second
series (64640, then 709120 forty seconds later, 2 of 43 reports). When a reference from one
series is paired with a pts from the other, pj_timestamp_diff32 truncates the genuinely
negative delta 709120 - 2177760970 = -2,177,051,850 into +2,117,915,446, which passes
av_sync's only guard (`diff <= 0`). Divided by the 16 kHz audio clock that becomes 36.77
hours of NTP, it is stored as max_ntp, and every subsequent audio frame then looks that far
behind -- producing speed-up requests of tens of millions of milliseconds.

Each media does convert with its own clock rate correctly (16000 audio, 90000 video); the
16 kHz divisor only amplifies the error 5.6x relative to video. The reference association
itself is what is wrong, and it is left for a separate change rather than papered over
here: this patch makes the video stream survive such a request instead of being killed by
it.

It also separates three things the encoder counter was conflating.

`counters.encoded` incremented whenever encode_begin() returned PJ_SUCCESS -- including the
calls that produced a zero-size frame because the component had no input buffer to give.
Read as a frame rate it said 30/s on an SM-M146B while the encoder was emitting 4.4
pictures/s, and the true figure was only visible in the codec's own ledger. A counter that
looks like a measurement and is not cost real time during this work, so the three
questions now have three answers: `encoded` (calls that succeeded), `encoded_frames` (calls
that produced a payload -- the rate) and `encoded_empty` (the difference, stated rather than
left to subtraction). The vidcnt line carries encf= and ence= beside enc=, and the reader
that parses it was updated with them.
